{%- comment -%}
  nav-header — mount point for the hardcoded Roberto Coin navigation (replaces Qikify).
  Renders the closed mobile bar, the desktop transparent header + mega-menu, and the drill-down drawer.
  Transparent overlay on every template (the bar sits over the page hero — no green band).

  @param {string} [default_mode] - Initial header colour scheme ('light' | 'dark'),
    from the header section's per-template header_default_mode. Drives data-scheme
    on first paint; nav-menu.dev.js's initScheme overrides it where observer sections exist.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%} nav-menu.min.css is generated — edit nav-menu.css, then run `npm run minify` {%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'nav-menu.min.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- liquid
  assign rc_transparent = 1
  assign rc_menu = section.settings.rc_menu_linklist | default: 'main-menu'
  assign rc_blocks = section.blocks

  # Mega-panel image parallax, off via the section's `rc_panel_parallax` switch.
  # nav-menu.dev.js reads data-parallax once at init; the CSS also neutralises the
  # transform so toggling in the theme editor shows immediately, before any reload.
  assign rc_parallax = '1'
  unless section.settings.rc_panel_parallax
    assign rc_parallax = '0'
  endunless

  # Initial colour scheme rendered server-side (correct on first paint, no flash).
  # Reuse the header's existing per-template header_default_mode (passed in as
  # default_mode). Where a page has [data-header-observer-mode] sections,
  # nav-menu.dev.js's initScheme takes over per scroll; where there are none it
  # early-returns and this value stays.
  assign rc_scheme = default_mode | default: 'dark'
-%}

<div class="rc-nav" data-transparent="{{ rc_transparent }}" data-scheme="{{ rc_scheme }}" data-parallax="{{ rc_parallax }}" data-open="0" data-search-url="{{ routes.search_url }}">
  {%- comment -%}
    data-header-height: the theme's setVars() (theme.js) measures the first
    [data-header-height] to publish --header-height / --content-full / --content-min.
    The legacy header carries it too but is display:none, so it measured 0. This bar
    is first in the DOM, so the theme reads the real header height from here instead.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="rc-nav__bar-wrap" data-header-height>

    {%- comment -%} closed mobile / tablet bar {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Mobile bar slots are plain (no rc-slide classes): the mobile directional
      slide animates the CONTENT inside each slot, and the left/right slots are
      their own overflow-clip containers — see .rc-mobile-bar__slot in nav-menu.css.
      (Desktop still uses .rc-slide--l/t/r on the .rc-d-* regions.)
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="rc-mobile-bar">
      <div class="rc-mobile-bar__slot">
        <button type="button" data-rc-drawer-open aria-label="Open menu">
          <svg width="26" height="26" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M3 9h18M3 15h18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3"></path></svg>
        </button>
      </div>
      <a class="rc-mobile-bar__slot" href="{{ routes.root_url }}" aria-label="{{ shop.name }} — home">
        <img class="rc-mobile-bar__logo" src="{{ 'nav-rc-logo-light.svg' | asset_url }}" alt="{{ shop.name }}" width="120" height="27">
      </a>
      <div class="rc-mobile-bar__slot">
        <a class="rc-m-bag" href="{{ routes.cart_url }}" data-cart-toggle aria-label="{{ 'cart.general.title' | t | default: 'Cart' }}">
          {%- render 'icon-cart' -%}
          <cart-count class="rc-m-bag__count" data-cart-count="{{ cart.item_count }}">{{ cart.item_count }}</cart-count>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Header breadcrumb, mobile copy — product / collection / page / search, same as the
      legacy .header__logo__meta. It sits outside .rc-mobile-bar (a fixed-height flex row)
      as its own row, mirroring how the legacy header stacked logo and meta. The desktop
      copy lives in nav-mega-desktop, inside .rc-desktop, which is display:none below
      1024px — hence one render per breakpoint. The wrapper is skipped when the snippet
      returns nothing, so templates without a crumb keep their header height.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- capture header_meta -%}{%- render 'nav-header-meta' -%}{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign header_meta = header_meta | strip -%}
    {%- if header_meta != blank -%}
      <div class="rc-mobile-crumbs">{{ header_meta }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- render 'nav-mega-desktop', rc_menu: rc_menu, blocks: rc_blocks -%}
  </div>

  {%- render 'nav-drawer-mobile', rc_menu: rc_menu, blocks: rc_blocks -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Search popdown, relocated here (out of the hidden legacy header) so `data-rc-search`
    can open the theme's real .search-popdown. The summary is the native <details> toggle;
    nav-menu.dev.js clicks it. unique:'rc' keeps element IDs distinct.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <header-search-popdown class="rc-search-popdown">
    <details>
      <summary class="rc-search-popdown__summary visually-hidden" data-rc-search-summary aria-haspopup="dialog" title="{{ 'general.search.search' | t }}">
        {%- render 'icon-search' -%}
      </summary>
      {%- render 'header-search-popdown', unique: 'rc' -%}
      <span class="underlay" data-popdown-underlay></span>
    </details>
  </header-search-popdown>
</div>
